> I'm using `rsconnect`:
> ```
> deployApp(appTitle='PROSPER - UK Adapatation Support', appName='prosper',
>           appFiles = c("lib/adaptation.R", "lib/synthesis.R", "lib/find_distribution.R",
>                        "app.R"))
> ```
> But it doesn't seem to include things that aren't in my appFiles list.
**assistant**:
> When using `rsconnect::deployApp()`, the `appFiles` parameter specifies exactly which files to include during deployment. If there are additional data files or folders that your app needs, you’ll need to explicitly include them in the `appFiles` list. Here’s how to do that:
> 
> ### Steps to Include Additional Files
> 
> 1. **Add Data Files to `appFiles`:**  
>    You can add your data files and directories directly to the `appFiles` vector. For example, if you have a `data` directory, modify your `deployApp` call like this:
>    ```r
>    deployApp(appTitle = 'PROSPER - UK Adaptation Support', appName = 'prosper',
>              appFiles = c("lib/adaptation.R", "lib/synthesis.R", "lib/find_distribution.R",
>                           "app.R", "data/file1.csv", "data/subfolder/file2.csv"))
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Use Wildcards (if appropriate):**  
>    If you have many files in a directory and want to include all of them without listing each one, you might consider using a wildcard (typically, this would be handled in a custom function as `rsconnect::deployApp()` does not natively support wildcards). For example:
>    ```r
>    data_files <- list.files("data", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
>    app_files <- c("lib/adaptation.R", "lib/synthesis.R", "lib/find_distribution.R", "app.R", data_files)
> 
>    deployApp(appTitle = 'PROSPER - UK Adaptation Support', appName = 'prosper', appFiles = app_files)
>    ```
> 
> 3. **Verify Your Path:**  
>    Make sure that the paths you specify correctly reflect where the files are located relative to your project directory.
> 
> 4. **Check `options(rsconnect.verbose = TRUE)`:**  
>    For more information during deployment, you can enable verbose mode to see which files are being considered during the deploy process:
>    ```r
>    options(rsconnect.verbose = TRUE)
>    deployApp(appTitle = 'PROSPER - UK Adaptation Support', appName = 'prosper', appFiles = app_files)
>    ```
> 
> ### Example of Full Code
> Here’s how your deployment code might look after including additional files:
> 
> ```r
> # List all data files to include
> data_files <- list.files("data", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
> # Combine all needed files into one list
> app_files <- c("lib/adaptation.R", "lib/synthesis.R", "lib/find_distribution.R", "app.R", data_files)
> 
> # Enable verbose output for debugging
> options(rsconnect.verbose = TRUE)
> 
> # Deploy the app
> deployApp(appTitle = 'PROSPER - UK Adaptation Support', appName = 'prosper', appFiles = app_files)
> ```
> 
> By following these steps, ensure your necessary internal files are included in the deployment. If you continue to experience issues, let me know!
**user**:
> How do I do wildcards in list.files?
**assistant**:
> In R, you can use the `pattern` argument of the `list.files()` function to specify a regular expression that acts like a wildcard. This allows you to filter the files you want to include. Here's how to do it:
> 
> ### Syntax
> 
> ```r
> list.files(path = "your_directory", pattern = "your_pattern", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
> ```
> 
> ### Examples
> 
> 1. **Including All Files in a Directory:**
>    If you want to include all files in a directory (e.g., all CSV files), you can use:
>    ```r
>    all_files <- list.files("data", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Including Specific File Types:**
>    For example, to include only CSV files:
>    ```r
>    csv_files <- list.files("data", pattern = "\\.csv$", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
>    ```
> 
>    - `pattern = "\\.csv$"`: This regex matches any file ending with `.csv`.
> 
> 3. **Including All Files Matching a Multiple Patterns:**
>    If you want to include all files that start with "file" and have any extension:
>    ```r
>    specific_files <- list.files("data", pattern = "^file.*", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
>    ```
> 
>    - `pattern = "^file.*"`: This regex matches any file starting with `file`.
> 
> ### Full Example in the Context of Your Shiny App
> 
> If you want to include all data files in the `data` subdirectory when deploying your Shiny app, you might write:
> 
> ```r
> # List all files in the data directory
> data_files <- list.files("data", full.names = TRUE, recursive = TRUE)
> 
> # Combine all needed files into one list for deployment
> app_files <- c("lib/adaptation.R", "lib/synthesis.R", "lib/find_distribution.R", "app.R", data_files)
> 
> # Deploy
> deployApp(appTitle = 'PROSPER - UK Adaptation Support', appName = 'prosper', appFiles = app_files)
> ```
> 
> This usage will ensure all files within the `data` folder (and its subfolders) are included in the deployment. If you have specific file types or naming conventions, you can always adjust the `pattern` argument accordingly. Let me know if you have any other questions!
